WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.157 --> 00:00:01.757
Hello.

2
00:00:01.617 --> 00:00:06.306
I'm Ana Morales, the revenue cycle manager at
Kestrel Falls Community Hospital.

3
00:00:06.306 --> 00:00:09.407
Laura Hemmingsen asked me to
explain what my team does,

4
00:00:09.407 --> 00:00:13.553
for people who don't work in finance, and
what the new EHR means for us.

5
00:00:13.553 --> 00:00:14.153
My world is claims.

6
00:00:13.553 --> 00:00:17.907
Every visit, every procedure, every lab
test turns into a charge,

7
00:00:17.907 --> 00:00:20.769
the coders assign the codes, and we
send the claim to the payer.

8
00:00:20.769 --> 00:00:23.966
That's Medicare, Medicaid, and the
commercial insurers.

9
00:00:23.966 --> 00:00:26.375
Then we post the payments and
follow up on denials.

10
00:00:26.375 --> 00:00:29.302
If that chain breaks anywhere,
the money stops.

11
00:00:29.302 --> 00:00:30.996
And it doesn't stop for a day.

12
00:00:30.996 --> 00:00:35.303
It stops for weeks, while we find the
problem, fix it, and resend.

13
00:00:35.303 --> 00:00:37.749
How big is that flow of money?

14
00:00:37.749 --> 00:00:41.276
We bill more than thirty million dollars a month
across the hospital, the clinics,

15
00:00:41.276 --> 00:00:42.438
and the ambulance service.

16
00:00:42.438 --> 00:00:44.805
That's what pays salaries and
buys supplies.

17
00:00:44.805 --> 00:00:47.294
So even a few weeks of delay is a lot of
cash that isn't coming in.

18
00:00:47.294 --> 00:00:52.549
My biggest concern is simple: claims stall and
revenue drops right after go-live.

19
00:00:52.549 --> 00:00:55.860
I've talked with revenue managers
at other hospitals,

20
00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.421
and many of them saw a cash dip
after their EHR switch.

21
00:00:58.421 --> 00:00:59.673
Some took months to recover.

22
00:00:59.673 --> 00:01:03.373
The bills go out late, or they go
out wrong and get denied,

23
00:01:03.373 --> 00:01:06.725
and you're fixing the past while
the present piles up.

24
00:01:06.725 --> 00:01:09.429
The chain usually breaks in a few places.

25
00:01:09.429 --> 00:01:11.802
Registration, if insurance information
doesn't come over right.

26
00:01:11.802 --> 00:01:16.025
Charges, if a department's services aren't
built correctly in the new system,

27
00:01:16.025 --> 00:01:18.978
so nothing drops to the bill.

28
00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:21.454
Coding, if my coders are slow because
they're learning new screens.

29
00:01:21.454 --> 00:01:25.952
And the connection to the payers, if the
claim format has an error.

30
00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:27.930
Any one of those stops claims.

31
00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:28.530
About my coders.

32
00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:32.577
We have fourteen coders, and right now
they're about six days behind.

33
00:01:32.577 --> 00:01:34.711
That's before any change.

34
00:01:34.711 --> 00:01:38.346
If they hit go-live already behind and
then slow down while they learn,

35
00:01:38.346 --> 00:01:39.779
the backlog grows fast.

36
00:01:39.779 --> 00:01:42.919
I need them trained early, and I need the
backlog down before we switch,

37
00:01:42.919 --> 00:01:47.916
because their work depends on what
the clinicians document.

38
00:01:47.916 --> 00:01:50.030
If the doctors' notes change format,

39
00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:52.843
my coders have to learn to find
things in a new place.

40
00:01:52.843 --> 00:01:56.183
What I need from the project is billing tested
before go-live, from start to finish.

41
00:01:56.183 --> 00:02:02.266
Not just "the screen loads." I mean: register
a test patient, document a visit,

42
00:02:02.266 --> 00:02:05.334
code it, build the claim, and send
it to a test payer,

43
00:02:05.334 --> 00:02:06.882
for every major payer we work with.

44
00:02:06.882 --> 00:02:10.585
If one payer rejects it, we
find out in testing,

45
00:02:10.585 --> 00:02:14.416
not in December when the money
doesn't come in.

46
00:02:14.416 --> 00:02:16.451
I also need something from
leadership, not just IT.

47
00:02:16.451 --> 00:02:17.669
A cash plan.

48
00:02:17.669 --> 00:02:20.673
I'd like Greg and the board to
agree, before go-live,

49
00:02:20.673 --> 00:02:21.757
on how we cover a slow few months.

50
00:02:21.757 --> 00:02:25.359
Maybe a line of credit, maybe
timing of big purchases.

51
00:02:25.359 --> 00:02:29.140
And I want daily claims numbers for
the first ninety days,

52
00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:31.197
so we see a problem in days,
not at month end.

53
00:02:31.197 --> 00:02:33.050
So where do I stand overall?

54
00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:34.928
I'm cautious, not against it.

55
00:02:34.928 --> 00:02:38.878
Plexmoor isn't an option much
longer, I understand that.

56
00:02:38.878 --> 00:02:43.358
I just want our finance people treated as
part of the clinical system,

57
00:02:43.358 --> 00:02:43.958
because we are.

58
00:02:43.358 --> 00:02:46.161
If we're in the room from the start,

59
00:02:46.161 --> 00:02:48.609
I think we can do this without the
hospital feeling it.

60
00:02:48.609 --> 00:02:52.896
The areas I'm especially worried about are the
ambulance billing and the clinics.

61
00:02:52.896 --> 00:02:54.983
Ambulance claims have their own rules,

62
00:02:54.983 --> 00:02:57.118
and the clinics bill differently
from the hospital.

63
00:02:57.118 --> 00:03:01.581
Those are the places where a small build mistake
can stop a whole stream of claims

64
00:03:01.581 --> 00:03:02.786
and nobody notices for weeks.

65
00:03:02.786 --> 00:03:04.639
I'd want those tested twice.

66
00:03:04.639 --> 00:03:09.320
And on my team, my senior coder and my billing
supervisor should be involved in the

67
00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:11.617
build, at least half time, starting early.

68
00:03:11.617 --> 00:03:13.441
They know where the problems hide.

69
00:03:13.441 --> 00:03:18.160
If they're only brought in at testing, it's
too late to fix the design cheaply.

